---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const structuredData = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "A guide to time zones and daylight saving",
  description:
    "How time zones, UTC offsets, and daylight saving actually work, and the specific limitations of TimeAtlas's clocks, search, and globe.",
};
---

<Layout
  title="Guide to time zones and daylight saving"
  description="How time zones, UTC offsets, and daylight saving actually work — and the specific limitations of TimeAtlas's clocks, search, and globe."
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-paper-100">
      A guide to time zones and daylight saving
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-paper-400">
      This is a plain explanation of how clocks around the world actually work, and an honest
      account of what TimeAtlas can and can't promise.
    </p>

    <nav aria-label="On this page" class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-navy-700 bg-navy-800/40 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="font-medium text-paper-200">On this page</p>
      <ul class="mt-2 grid gap-1 text-paper-400 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#time-zones">What a time zone actually is</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#utc">UTC and offsets</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#half-quarter">Half- and quarter-hour zones</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#dst">Daylight saving time</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#multi-zone">Countries with more than one time zone</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#accuracy">How accurate are TimeAtlas's clocks?</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#coverage">Dataset coverage</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#globe">What the globe does and doesn't show</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <section class="prose-section mt-8">
      <h2 id="time-zones" class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">What a time zone actually is</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        A time zone is a region that agrees to use the same clock. It isn't a strip of
        longitude — political borders, not meridians, decide where one zone ends and the next
        begins, which is why zone boundaries zigzag along country and state lines rather than
        running in straight vertical bands. The rules for a given zone — its offset from UTC,
        whether and when it observes daylight saving — are maintained by the{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.iana.org/time-zones"
          class="underline hover:text-paper-100"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank">IANA Time Zone Database</a
        >, usually called tzdata. Every clock on TimeAtlas is computed from an IANA zone
        identifier (like <code class="tabular">Europe/Berlin</code> or{" "}
        <code class="tabular">America/Argentina/Cordoba</code>), not a hand-entered offset, so
        it automatically picks up the real historical and current rules for that place.
      </p>

      <h2 id="utc" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">UTC and offsets</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        UTC (Coordinated Universal Time) is the reference every zone is defined relative to.
        "UTC+5:30" means local time is five and a half hours ahead of UTC at that instant.
        Offsets aren't fixed forever: a zone's offset can change when it starts or ends
        daylight saving, and — far less often — when a country changes its standard time by
        law. TimeAtlas always computes a zone's offset for the specific instant being shown,
        never a single constant baked in ahead of time.
      </p>

      <h2 id="half-quarter" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">
        Half- and quarter-hour zones
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Most zones sit on a whole-hour offset, but not all of them. India and Sri Lanka use
        UTC+5:30; Newfoundland uses UTC&minus;3:30; Nepal uses UTC+5:45; parts of Australia use
        UTC+9:30 or UTC+8:45. These aren't rounding artifacts — they're real, deliberately
        chosen offsets, and TimeAtlas displays them exactly (down to the minute) rather than
        rounding to the nearest hour.
      </p>

      <h2 id="dst" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Daylight saving time</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Daylight saving time (DST) shifts clocks forward for part of the year, usually to
        align waking hours with daylight in summer. Not every country observes it — most of
        Asia and Africa don't — and the ones that do don't all switch on the same dates, or
        even in the same direction at the same time of year: the Southern Hemisphere's
        "summer" DST period falls in what's winter north of the equator.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        TimeAtlas determines whether a given instant falls in daylight saving by finding the
        zone's real nearest clock-offset change before and after that instant and comparing
        them — the daylight-saving offset is always the larger of the two — rather than by
        checking a couple of fixed sample dates. If a zone has had no offset change in the
        past or next year (true for zones that don't use DST at all), we show the UTC offset
        without a daylight-saving claim either way, instead of guessing.
      </p>

      <h2 id="multi-zone" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">
        Countries with more than one time zone
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Large countries — Russia, the United States, Australia, Brazil, Indonesia, Canada,
        and dozens more — span several time zones. There is no single "Russia time" or "USA
        time." When you select one of these countries on TimeAtlas, we never show you one
        city's clock as if it spoke for the whole country: we list its zones and let you pick
        a specific city in the one you care about.
      </p>

      <h2 id="accuracy" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">
        How accurate are TimeAtlas's clocks?
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        The "Live" clock on TimeAtlas reads your device's own system clock — it is not
        synchronized against an atomic clock or a time server, and it is not independently
        verified in any way. If your device's clock is wrong, every "Live" time on this site
        will be off by the same amount. We resynchronize from the system clock every second,
        and immediately again whenever the tab becomes visible or the device wakes from sleep,
        so the display won't drift or freeze — but it is only ever as accurate as your device.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Time zone and daylight-saving rules are set by law and occasionally change — a
        country can adopt, abolish, or shift its daylight-saving schedule with real-world
        notice measured in months, not years. TimeAtlas relies on the time zone database
        built into your browser (via the Intl JavaScript API), which is updated as part of
        normal browser updates; a rule change that's very recent may not be reflected until
        your browser updates its own copy of that database.
      </p>

      <h2 id="coverage" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Dataset coverage</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        TimeAtlas's city search covers every city with a GeoNames population record of
        15,000 or more, plus every national capital regardless of size, plus at least one
        representative city for every (country, time zone) combination that occurs in that
        data, plus the largest known city for any remaining country with no other entry. That
        is a broad, genuinely useful set — a little over 12,000 places — but it is
        <strong>not</strong> a complete list of the world's settlements, and it is not a
        gazetteer. If a small town isn't in our search, try the nearest larger city or the
        country itself. A handful of uninhabited or near-uninhabited territories (Antarctica,
        Bouvet Island, the British Indian Ocean Territory, Tokelau, and the U.S. Minor
        Outlying Islands) have no permanent population for us to list a city for at all; we
        show their time zone(s) without a city.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Very rarely, a source record's time zone assignment is itself slightly off (for
        example, a town very close to a time-zone boundary). We correct specific, confirmed
        cases like this by pointing to the right IANA zone identifier for that place — never
        by inventing a fixed offset. See{" "}
        <a href="/about/#data-sources" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">data sources</a>{" "}
        for exactly where this data comes from and its license.
      </p>

      <h2 id="globe" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">
        What the globe does and doesn't show
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        The interactive globe is a simplified 3D model, not satellite imagery or a live feed
        of Earth. Its day/night shading is an approximation of where the sun is overhead,
        calculated from standard low-precision solar-position formulas — good for an
        intuitive sense of "it's night there right now," not a substitute for a real sunrise/
        sunset calculator. Country borders are simplified for performance and come from
        Natural Earth's public-domain dataset, which documents its own conventions for
        disputed and de facto boundaries; TimeAtlas draws them as-is and does not intend them
        as a political statement. If your browser or device can't run WebGL, or the 3D
        context is lost mid-session, TimeAtlas falls back to a flat 2D map — every time,
        search, and comparison feature keeps working either way.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
